  "summary": "I wrote the solar-system explorer in src/main.ts, but it is unverified. `@aura3d/engine` is missing from the sandbox: `node_modules/@aura3d/` is empty, so `typecheck` can't resolve the import. The only reported errors are TS2307 (cannot find module '@aura3d/engine') and one TS7006 that follows from it (`f` in `onFrame` has no inferred type). Nothing has been built or run in a browser. I wrote it against `llms.txt` and `docs/agents/api-signatures.md` only. Several option names (for example `opacity` on `material.emissive`, `distance` and `decay` on `lights.point`, and the torus ring sizing) are guesses and may need fixing once the package is installed.\n\n**Scene:**\n- **Star:** an emissive star with a glow shell, plus bloom.\n- **Planets:** 8 planets, Mercury to Neptune, with different distances, sizes and colours. Inner planets orbit faster, using a compressed period law with Earth's year at 24 s. Each planet spins and has surface spots so the spin is visible.\n- **Moons and ring:** a moon for Earth, two for Jupiter, and a two-band ring on Saturn.\n- **Orbit lines:** a dotted circle for each planet.\n- **Background:** a procedural starfield on a dark background.\n\n**Camera:** `app.camera`'s signature isn't documented, so I didn't use it. The engine camera stays fixed at the origin and I move the world through nested groups for distance, pitch, yaw and pan. The point light moves with the star. Overview drag orbits and scroll zooms (distance 55 to 300).\n\n**Focus:** a click on a planet starts a 1.5 s eased move to a close view, then follows the planet. The panel shows its name, orbital period in Earth days and distance in million km. Clicking empty space or pressing Escape returns to the overview. Picking uses my own screen-space hit test, with a minimum 10 px radius so small planets are clickable. The group rotations assume three.js-style Euler conventions, so if the engine's signs differ, clicks will miss the planets.\n\n**Controls and state:** the 